  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::6558",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride (KCl) exhibits a strong binding interaction with multiple members of the Slc12 family, specifically Slc12a5, Slc12a6, and Slc12a7, which are all involved in cation-coupled chloride cotransport. This binding suggests a potential role for KCl in modulating the activity of these transporters. The shared participation of these transporters in cation-coupled chloride transport implies that KCl may influence the transport process by altering the electrochemical gradient or by interacting with the transporter's regulatory domains. The presence of a common downstream pathway involving Slc12a2, which is also a cation-coupled chloride transporter, further supports the hypothesis that KCl may affect Slc12a2 through these shared mechanisms. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust and reliable relationship, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence suggests that KCl may act as a modulator of Slc12a2 function, potentially through direct binding or by influencing the activity of the broader cation-coupled chloride transport system.",
